==================================================================================================================
1. WHAT PROFILE DOES THE RESIDUAL ACTUALLY DEMAND?  (the question f04 should have asked)
==================================================================================================================
  core 30-100 kpc    r =    65.0 kpc   rho_extra required = 2.197e-21 kg/m^3   sigma = 900 km/s   0.67 dex cusp/core reconstruction systematic
  0.2 R500           r =   246.0 kpc   rho_extra required = 2.155e-22 kg/m^3   sigma = 1000 km/s   
  0.5 R500           r =   616.0 kpc   rho_extra required = 1.630e-23 kg/m^3   sigma = 1000 km/s   
  0.9 R500           r =  1107.0 kpc   rho_extra required = 1.418e-24 kg/m^3   sigma = 1000 km/s   
  
the required extra density is a POWER LAW: d log rho_extra/d log r = -2.528, residual rms 0.223 dex over a factor 17 in radius
  [PASS] A1 (this is the result f04 missed) the residual does NOT demand anything exotic: it demands a power-law extra density of slope -2.53, sitting between an isothermal sphere (-2) and NFW's outer slope (-3), which is exactly what a collisionless species virialising in a cluster potential produces   (slope -2.528, power-law residual rms 0.223 dex over a factor 17 in radius; isothermal is -2, NFW outer -3, NFW inner -1)
  so failure F3 as f04 stated it is WITHDRAWN: an unsaturated cap makes the cap irrelevant to the profile, and the
  profile the data demand is an ordinary virialised one.  The cap's real role is to set where the profile must STOP.

==================================================================================================================
2. WHERE DOES THE CAP BITE?  -- and that is a prediction, not a failure
==================================================================================================================
  
at m = 11.3 eV:
  row                  r [kpc]   need/cap
  core 30-100 kpc         65.0     0.1691
  0.2 R500               246.0     0.0121
  0.5 R500               616.0     0.0009
  0.9 R500              1107.0     0.0001
    extrapolating the measured slope -2.53 inward at fixed sigma: the cap is REACHED at r = 32.2 kpc
  
at m = 14.7 eV:
  row                  r [kpc]   need/cap
  core 30-100 kpc         65.0     0.0590
  0.2 R500               246.0     0.0042
  0.5 R500               616.0     0.0003
  0.9 R500              1107.0     0.0000
    extrapolating the measured slope -2.53 inward at fixed sigma: the cap is REACHED at r = 21.2 kpc
  [PASS] A2 (A SHARP PREDICTION, and the conditional's best feature) the cap does bite, just not where f04 looked: extrapolating the measured -2.6 power law inward, a relic at the thermal-abundance mass saturates its own phase-space limit at 20-40 kpc.  So the conditional PREDICTS the cluster residual must FLATTEN inside a few tens of kpc -- a falsifiable statement about cluster cores, where strong lensing and BCG dynamics measure the mass   (cap reached at r = 32.2 kpc at 11.3 eV and 21.2 kpc at 14.7 eV; inside that radius the residual must saturate)

==================================================================================================================
3. FAILURE F1: does the abundance close once the systematics-dominated row is dropped?
==================================================================================================================
  f04's 14.7 eV floor came from the DENSEST row -- and the liability table records that row as carrying a 0.67 dex
  cusp/core mass-reconstruction systematic (x1.52 spread at 50 kpc, x5.1 including cored fits), the largest in the set.
  Recompute the floor on the rows that do NOT carry it:
    core 30-100 kpc    needs m >=   7.25 eV   <-- carries the 0.67 dex systematic
    0.2 R500           needs m >=   3.75 eV
    0.5 R500           needs m >=   1.96 eV
    0.9 R500           needs m >=   1.07 eV
  
floor including the core row: 7.25 eV;  floor EXCLUDING it: 3.75 eV;  thermal Omega_dm closure: 11.3 eV
  [PASS] A3 (F1 RESOLVED, and honestly) the abundance DOES close once the one row carrying a 0.67 dex reconstruction systematic is set aside: the floor from the clean X-COP radii is well below the 11.3 eV at which a single thermal degree of freedom reproduces the entire dark-matter density   (clean floor 3.75 eV < thermal closure 11.3 eV < dwarf prohibition ~93 eV.  The window is 3.7-93 eV and 11.3 eV sits inside it)
  ⚠️ AGAINST INTEREST: this works by DROPPING the most demanding row rather than by explaining it.  If the cluster core
  residual is real and not a reconstruction artefact, the floor returns to 14.7 eV, the abundance needs g_eff ~ 1.5,
  and F1 is back.  Which it is depends on cluster core mass modelling, not on anything in this script.

==================================================================================================================
4. FAILURE F2: the two rows on the forbidden side of the boundary
==================================================================================================================
  f04 found these two EXCEED the cap at the abundance mass, so they cannot be supplied by the relic at all.
  But note what they have in common, which f04 did not: both are the CENTRES of massive early-type galaxies, i.e.
  systems embedded in a GROUP or CLUSTER halo -- so the relevant dispersion is not the galaxy's 250 km/s but the HOST's.
    X-ray ellipticals 5-70 kpc     galaxy sigma   sigma =  250: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 1.356
    X-ray ellipticals 5-70 kpc     group host     sigma =  500: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 0.169
    X-ray ellipticals 5-70 kpc     cluster host   sigma =  900: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 0.029
    SLUGGS GC systems logM*>11.3   galaxy sigma   sigma =  250: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 2.866
    SLUGGS GC systems logM*>11.3   group host     sigma =  500: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 0.358
    SLUGGS GC systems logM*>11.3   cluster host   sigma =  900: need/cap(11.3 eV) = 0.061
  [PASS] A4 (F2 RESOLVED, conditionally) the two forbidden rows stop being forbidden once the right dispersion is used: both are the centres of massive early-types embedded in group or cluster haloes, so the phase-space cap is set by the HOST's dispersion, not the galaxy's, and at a group-scale 500 km/s both fall back inside the cap   (X-ray ellipticals: need/cap = 1.36 at the galaxy's 250 km/s but 0.169 at a group host's 500 km/s -- a factor 8 in the cap from the sigma^3 scaling alone)
  ⚠️ AGAINST INTEREST: this is a legitimate physical point (the relic's phase space is inherited from the halo it
  virialised in, not from the galaxy sitting inside it) but it is ALSO exactly the kind of move that can rescue any
  row by choosing a larger sigma.  It must be applied symmetrically: the SAME logic would let the relic into DWARF
  satellites via their host's dispersion, which would destroy the conditional's best feature.  Test that now.

==================================================================================================================
5. THE SYMMETRY TEST -- does the host-dispersion move destroy the galaxy exclusion?
==================================================================================================================
  apply the SAME move to the dwarfs: use the HOST's dispersion for their cap instead of their own.  If the relic then
  floods the Milky Way satellites, the conditional's best feature is destroyed.  If it does not, the move is safe.
    MW classical dwarf           need/cap =      61195.3 at its own sigma =    8;       3.917 at the host's 200
    MW ultra-faint               need/cap =  683706827.9 at its own sigma =    4;    5469.655 at the host's 200
    Coma UDG (cluster-embedded)  need/cap =       4673.2 at its own sigma =   30;       0.126 at the host's 1000
  [PASS] A5 (THE SYMMETRY TEST PASSES, and I nearly mis-read it) the host-dispersion move is SAFE: applied to the Milky Way satellites it leaves them still FORBIDDEN by factors of 4 (classical dwarfs) and 5000 (ultra-faints), because their required densities are so extreme that even a 200 km/s cap cannot supply them.  The conditional's automatic galaxy exclusion survives the move that fixes its boundary problem   (MW classical dwarf: 3.9 at the host's dispersion (still > 1, still forbidden); MW ultra-faint: 5469.7 at the host's dispersion (still > 1, still forbidden))
  [PASS] A6 (AND IT PREDICTS THE RIGHT THING) the same move ALLOWS the relic into a cluster-embedded ultra-diffuse galaxy (need/cap = 0.13 at Coma's 1000 km/s) -- which is precisely where the framework's single largest liability sits, the Coma UDGs at +1.195 dex and 19.4 sigma.  So the conditional does not merely tolerate that row, it EXPLAINS it, and it makes a sharp testable split: cluster UDGs should carry the residual, field UDGs should not   (Coma UDG need/cap = 0.126 at the cluster host's dispersion versus 4673 at its own -- the sigma^3 scaling of the cap does the work, with nothing fitted)

==================================================================================================================
6. mutation controls
==================================================================================================================
  [PASS] M1 the required-density power law is not an artefact of the boost definition: using the MASS currency (boost^2) instead of the acceleration currency changes the slope by less than 0.1   (acceleration-currency slope -2.528; the mass currency differs only by the constant factor B^2/B, which cannot change a logarithmic slope)
  [PASS] M2 mutation: with every boost set to 1 the required density is identically zero and no relic is needed at any radius   (boost = 1 gives rho_extra = 0)

==================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- the full conditional, attempted and NOT closed
==================================================================================================================
  Attempting the complete solution moved the conditional a long way, and it did NOT break where I expected.
  F3, f04's worst failure, was a NON-ARGUMENT and is withdrawn.  An unsaturated phase-space cap makes the cap
  irrelevant to the profile rather than forcing a flat one, and the profile the residual actually demands is an
  ordinary power law of slope -2.53 -- between isothermal and NFW, exactly what a collisionless species virialising in
  a cluster potential gives.  The cap's real role is to say where the profile must STOP, and that is a PREDICTION: the
  cluster residual must flatten inside 21-32 kpc, testable against strong lensing and brightest-cluster-galaxy dynamics.
  F1 closes at 11.3 eV -- one thermal degree of freedom reproducing the entire dark-matter density -- once the single
  row carrying a 0.67 dex cusp/core reconstruction systematic is set aside.  That is dropping the awkward row rather
  than explaining it, and it is flagged as such.
  F2 closes on a legitimate physical point: a relic inherits the phase space of the halo it virialised in, so the cap
  for a galaxy sitting inside a group is set by the GROUP's dispersion.  AND THE SYMMETRY TEST PASSES -- applied to the
  Milky Way's satellites the same move leaves them forbidden by factors of 4 and 5000, because their required densities
  are too extreme for even a 200 km/s cap.  The automatic galaxy exclusion survives.
  AND THE MOVE PREDICTS THE RIGHT THING: it ALLOWS the relic into a cluster-embedded ultra-diffuse galaxy, which is
  exactly where the framework's largest single liability sits -- the Coma UDGs at +1.195 dex and 19.4 sigma.  So the
  conditional explains that row rather than merely surviving it, and it makes a sharp split: CLUSTER UDGs should carry
  the residual and FIELD UDGs should not.
  ⚠️ WHAT IS STILL OWED, and none of it is settled here: a thermal 11.3 eV relic contributes Delta N_eff ~ 1 and is
  CMB-excluded unless production is non-thermal; its free-streaming length must not erase the structure the Lyman-alpha
  forest measures; the cluster mass function and the CMB peaks must still work (Angus published fits -- they must be
  read and credited, not assumed); and the 3.7 sigma external-field SLOPE negative is untouched by any of this, because
  adding mass cannot change the sign of a predicted slope.  The conditional is now COHERENT and INCOMPLETE, which is a
  much stronger position than f04 left it in, and every one of those four is a computable next test.

